A||||6||Hawk||coil over rear suspension for 87 GT||||1||
Z||000000||Hawk||11-16-2000||12:08 PM||||I am interested in installing a rear coil over set up on myu 87 GT, I have searched some of the past posts but I am unclear on certain things,  what is the most cost effective method? Do I need to install the 88 cradle assembly? Can I buy aftermarket coil overs and attach tem to my 87 cradle? Thanks for any help!||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||11-16-2000||12:31 PM||||Hawk,<P>The better question is, what are you trying to achieve the the coil-overs?<P>Yes... coil-overs can easily be mounted to any Fiero including your 87 cradle.  <P>No... you do not need an 88 cradle.<P>What is the most cost effective method is to make your own kit.  They really are simple and I have been kicking myself for purchasing them for a while now.  Of course they are not that expensive (Hypnotise sells GC's for $200).  <P>I couldn't imagine any advantage of adapting coil-overs from another factory car.  <P>I hope this answers some of the questions you were undecided about.    <P>William T. Thorn, III<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||HYPTNOTiSE||11-16-2000||12:31 PM||||The coilovers that i sell are without a doubt the most cost effective of any coil-overs. They take the place of the spring on any rear fiero strut. No modifications to the cradle or chassis. They use all stock brake components and suspension components. You can also use these coilovers on KYB's, Koni's, Bilstiens(if you can find em  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) Cheezy lowering springs will cost you at least 80-100 bucks. For twice that cost of $199, you get 2 Eibach springs, And the rest of the hardware to modify your strut into a coilover.  You get full adjustability of ride height and you can take your pick of spring rates when ordering. Spring rates depend on your sytle of driving and which engine is installed in the car. Springs can also be purchased seperately so that you could have one set of springs for the track and one for the street.  Nice setup, let me know if you would like a set for your fiero or almost any other popular car.<P>------------------<BR>       Mike LeCompte<BR>86 Fiero GT 3.2L w/ Nitrous<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tcac.net/~daddyg/hypsig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hawk||11-16-2000||01:03 PM||||Thanks for the help, I am trying to get an over all stiffer and lower suspension. Correct me if I am wrong but won't coilovers  allow me to adjust the height of the suspension in the back? or do you just buy lower springs? William, fabricating the same set up that Hyptnotise sells would take some machining or lathe work no? Finally the $200 bucks seems reasonable my plan would be to lower the front and install koni or kyb all around depending on budget. I drive mostly aggressive street but enjoy track time when I can get it. P.S. are the koni shocks and strut for this car adjustable?<BR>Hawk||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||11-16-2000||08:42 PM||||Yes, the Koni's are adjustable, that's the only justification for spending that much for them. Bilstein is really excellent too, although not adjustable. KYB are right up there too.<P>A lower spring will work too, but the height and spring rate is fixed. With coil-overs, the spring rate choices are wide open and the height is easily adjustable.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||11-16-2000||09:35 PM||||hypnotise, i kind of started a coilover project a while back. got the instructions from one of the short lived fiero mags. i brought struts and cut and ground down the lower spring perch already. can i use your kit or dose yours use the spring perch as the bottom. i've seen it done once that way (kind of defeated part of the purpose of it for me. along with height and ride control i want the small spring diameter space for bigger wheel options). also i noticed that i can push one strut much easier than the other. i called gabriel (thats what they are) and was told that there are variances and i shouldnt worry. i may still buy a third one (can't take the cut ones back) and use the two that seem closest pressure wise. do you think thats nessesary?<P>dose yours come with the springs and threaded collars? what else might i have to get or is it compleat? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||HYPTNOTiSE||11-17-2000||01:21 AM||||Philipine, i'm going to have to check to see how my kit is installed, i believe that the spring perch on the strut is removed. I haven't recieved my first kit yet to tell you the truth but i'm going to take picture of the install and post them on my webpage along with instructions.  I'm going to buy the first kit, but if the top mounting plate is simle enough to fabricate, i may just make my own coilovers from here on out for the fiero. We'll see and i definately will keep everyone posted. Thanks!<P>Mike LeComtpe||||reg||1
